// kuipa -- Tamarin model of the enrollment protocol (docs/DESIGN.md)
//
// Scope (core exchange):
//   S4.2  anchors, claims, vouches, delegations
//   S4.3  realm advertisement and VerifiedRealm::verify
//   S4.4  enrollment string (pin + OTP, out of band)
//   S5.1  join key schedule (ML-KEM ss, ephemeral ss_e, transcript T)
//   S5.3  authenticator type 1 (OTP), the --ldap-otp shape
//   S5.5  response payload (keytab + anchors)
//   S6    rekey with the Kerberos authenticator (host keytab)
//   S7    sealed keytab: escrow registration and recovery
//   S7.6  user seals: two shares, the unified recovery request,
//         the answer encrypted to the device key, device/escrow
//         compromise (S7.6.10)
//   S9    vouchers: blind issue, bound and bearer use, single use
//         (trust establishment, S17.4, is modeled separately in
//         trust.spthy, as tang-pqc is)
//   S13   security analysis rows for the anchor-rooted flows
//
// Symbolic abstraction:
//   - ML-DSA  -> sgn(m, sk) with verification key vk(sk).  Verification
//     is modeled by matching the signature term against key-mapping
//     facts honest parties hold (the attacker cannot invert sgn).
//   - ML-KEM  -> kem_ct(ek, s) binds a fresh secret s under
//     encapsulation key ek = kem_pk(seed); only kem_dec(kem_sk(seed), .)
//     recovers s.  A vouch's Merkle tree over claims is abstracted to a
//     signature over each claim: the inclusion proof verifies iff the
//     anchor signed a root covering that claim, so the trust relation
//     is unchanged.
//   - AES-256-GCM (one message per key, nonce 0) -> senc/sdec.
//   - HKDF/H -> h(.).  The full transcript hash T is abstracted away:
//     each key derivation input list carries the same fields (op, keys,
//     identities), which is what T binds, so binding is preserved as a
//     term relation rather than as a hash chain (see the note at each
//     rule: "T binds ...").
//   - Time is not modeled: vouch, advertisement and pin expiries are
//     treated as unexpired (see DESIGN S13 "Clock").
//   - The voucher issuer serves only provisioners it can authenticate
//     (BlindReq is the provisioner's own record);  a rogue provisioner
//     with issuer access is out of scope -- the quota and one-more
//     unforgeability named in S9 are the mitigations.  Blindness is
//     structural: the issuer's only input is the hash mu.
//   - The seal descriptor signature (S7.6.3) and the revocation
//     windows are client-cache and time properties, outside the
//     symbolic model (S13 "Clock");  the registered device list is
//     the trusted fact !Device.  One server slot and one device slot
//     abstract the per-server and per-device lists of IPUF.
//
// Modeling notes:
//   - Receive rules take attacker-controlled fields as plain variables
//     and check them against honest state facts (ValidOtp, ValidHostKey)
//     by unification in linear premises; no deep term is pattern-matched
//     inside In(), which keeps the source constraints finite.
//   - The server's advertisement is a persistent fact the client reads
//     from (the network delivery is the Out of serve_adv); the client
//     verifies it by unifying with !Server, which is the symbolic form
//     of VerifiedRealm::verify (the pin must name this anchor, the
//     claim must name this server's signing key, and only the join key
//     of this server is used).
//   - Decryption is fact-driven (the "idealized decryption" idiom):
//     the party that sealed a payload records it in a linear fact
//     (Encap, SealedReq, JoinRsp, RekeyRsp) that only the honest
//     receiver consumes; wire terms carry the ciphertexts for the
//     attacker's view.  Every fact is created by the party that knows
//     the key, so this is sound for the properties proved here and
//     keeps the proof search terminating.
//
// Running the model:
//   tamarin-prover --prove kuipa.spthy --derivcheck-timeout=120
//
// Results with tamarin-prover 1.12.0 and Maude 3.5.1:
//   Exec, OtpSecret, KeytabSecret, JoinServerAuth,
//   RekeyForwardSecrecy, SealedNoOffline, SsSecretSource,
//   AttackerSsOnlyFromRogueSeeds, Attack_EscrowKeyOpensDisk,
//   VoucherExec, VoucherSingleUse, VoucherIssuerAuth,
//   BoundVoucherKeytabSecret, SealExec, SealReleaseRegistered,
//   SealNeedsDeviceKey: verified.
//   Attack_RoguePinLearnsOtp, Attack_StolenAnchorLearnsOtp: falsified,
//     the traces DESIGN S13 predicts for a mis-delivered enrollment
//     string and a stolen anchor; they show the secrecy lemmas are
//     conditioned on the honest pin, not vacuous.  EscrowKeyOpensDisk
//     being verified is correct: the DEK needs both shares (S7.6), so
//     the escrow key alone yields only the server share.
//   Attack_UnboundVoucherIsBearer,
//   Attack_EscrowKeyYieldsServerShare,
//   Attack_StolenDevicePlusReleaseOpensSeal: falsified, the S9/S7.6.10
//     rows -- a bearer voucher enrolls whoever holds it, the escrow
//     key yields the server share, and a stolen device key plus an
//     observed unlock answer opens the file.

theory kuipa
begin

functions: sgn/2, vk/1, kem_pk/1, kem_sk/1, kem_ct/2, kem_dec/2, senc/2, sdec/2, h/1
equations: kem_dec(kem_sk(x), kem_ct(kem_pk(x), s)) = s,
           sdec(senc(m, k), k) = m

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Server setup (S4.1, S11.2): one fact binds a server to all its own
// keys -- the anchor, the signing key and the join key.  The rogue
// variant gives the attacker its own keys (a forged enrollment string
// brings its own anchor, DESIGN S13).
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule server_setup:
  [ Fr(~ask), Fr(~ssk), Fr(~jseed) ]
  --[ HonestAnchor(vk(~ask)), HonestServer($srv, vk(~ask)) ]->
  [ !Server($srv, ~ask, vk(~ask), ~ssk, vk(~ssk), ~jseed, kem_pk(~jseed)) ]

rule rogue_server:
  [ Fr(~ask), Fr(~ssk), Fr(~jseed) ]
  -->
  [ !Server('rogue', ~ask, vk(~ask), ~ssk, vk(~ssk), ~jseed, kem_pk(~jseed)),
    !RogueJoinSeed(~jseed),
    Out(~ask), Out(~ssk), Out(~jseed) ]
  // !RogueJoinSeed marks the join-key seeds the attacker holds; the
  // attacker may run many rogue servers, and each exposes its seed

// The server publishes its advertisement: the delegation (claim signed
// by the anchor, advertisement signed by the signing key the claim
// names) and the join key.  This is what GET /ipa/ku/adv returns.
rule serve_adv:
  [ !Server(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek) ]
  -->
  [ !Adv(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek),
    Out( sgn(<srv, ek, svk, sgn(<srv, svk, avk>, ask)>, ssk) ) ]

// A thief who steals an honest anchor's private key (S4.7 "Limits"):
// the thief can now sign anything with it, but old vouches and the
// registration stay.  Lemmas that need an unstolen root condition on
// the absence of StolenAnchor.
rule steal_anchor:
  [ !Server(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek) ]
  --[ StolenAnchor(avk) ]->
  [ Out(ask) ]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Enrollment string (S4.4): the pin (anchor thumbprint) and the OTP
// reach the host out of band and authentically.  enroll_string pins an
// honest server's anchor; enroll_string_rogue pins the attacker's (a
// mis-delivered string).  ValidOtp is the directory's one-time record.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule enroll_string:
  [ !Server(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek), Fr(~otp) ]
  --[ Enroll($host, $fqdn, ~otp, avk) ]->
  [ ValidOtp($fqdn, ~otp),
    C0($host, $fqdn, ~otp, avk) ]

rule enroll_string_rogue:
  [ !Server('rogue', ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek), Fr(~otp) ]
  --[ Enroll($host, $fqdn, ~otp, avk) ]->
  [ ValidOtp($fqdn, ~otp),
    C0($host, $fqdn, ~otp, avk) ]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Client: advertisement verification and join request (S4.3, S5.1).
//
// The client accepts only an advertisement whose delegation vouch is
// signed by the pinned anchor and whose signing key belongs to the
// server that made it -- the !Adv premise unified against the pin in
// C0 is VerifiedRealm::verify.  The request:
//   ct   = kem_ct(ek, ss)            ss fresh (ML-KEM encapsulation)
//   ek_e = kem_pk(~eseed)            client's ephemeral KEM key
//   k    = h(ss, 'req', op, ek, svk, fqdn, ek_e)   -- T binds these
//   body = senc(<otp, reg>, k)       the OTP sealed under k
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule client_join:
  [ C0(host, fqdn, otp, avk),
    !Adv(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek),
    Fr(~ss), Fr(~eseed) ]
  // ek belongs to the server the pin vouches for; for the rogue server
  // the attacker holds jseed, hence kem_sk, hence ss
  --[ ClientReq(host, fqdn, ~ss, srv, ek, h(svk)) ]->
  [ C1(host, fqdn, avk, srv, ek, ~ss, ~eseed, svk),
    Encap(fqdn, kem_ct(ek, ~ss), ~ss),
    SealedReq(fqdn, kem_ct(ek, ~ss), otp),
    Out( <'join', ek, srv, fqdn, kem_ct(ek, ~ss), kem_pk(~eseed),
          senc(otp, h(~ss))> ) ]
  // The request body is the OTP sealed under the key derived from ss
  // (S5.1); the fresh ss stands for that key material.  Opening it
  // needs ss, which needs the join key's private half: honest servers
  // keep it, the rogue server's is public.  The server-side rule
  // consumes SealedReq (the idealized decryption) rather than
  // pattern-matching the ciphertext, which keeps the sources finite.
  // Encap records the client's encapsulation: ct = kem_ct(ek, ss) with
  // the secret ss.  The honest server reads ss from this record when
  // answering the ciphertext on the network (decapsulation by fact);
  // the attacker cannot forge it, and only the holder of kem_sk(jseed)
  // could learn ss from ct itself.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Server: decapsulation, authenticator check, response (S5.1-S5.3).
//
//   ss = Decaps(dk_kid, ct);  recompute k;  open the body;
//   check the OTP against the directory (one-time: ValidOtp consumed);
//   encapsulate to ek_e (forward secrecy);  the response key
//   k' = h(ss || ss_e, 'rsp', T') -- modeled as h(<ss, sse, 'rsp'>).
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule server_join:
  [ !Adv(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek),
    In( <'join', ek, srv, fqdn, ct, ek_e> ),
    Encap(fqdn, ct, ss),
    SealedReq(fqdn, ct, otp),
    ValidOtp(fqdn, otp),
    Fr(~sse), Fr(~keys) ]
  --[ ServerAccept(srv, fqdn, ~keys) ]->
  [ JoinRsp(fqdn, ek_e, ~sse, ss, ~keys, avk),
    Out( <'rsp', 'join', srv, kem_ct(ek_e, ~sse)> ) ]
  // the server decapsulates the network ciphertext ct to ss -- modeled
  // by the client's Encap record, which pairs ct with the ss only the
  // client that made it (and the join-key holder, by the KEM equation)
  // can know
  // ss is bound by the KEM equation: the ciphertext in the request is
  // kem_ct(ek, ss), and the honest server decapsulates it to ss (only
  // it can: kem_sk(jseed) is server-private).  The sealed body's key
  // h(<ss, 'req'>) re-derives from the same ss, which is the T-binding
  // of S5.1;  the OTP inside is checked against ValidOtp (the
  // directory's one-time record).

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Client: open the response (S5.5).
//
//   ss_e = Decaps(dk_e, ct_e);  k' = h(ss || ss_e, 'rsp');
//   payload: the keytab and the trusted anchors (the next pins, S4.5).
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule client_finish:
  [ C1(host, fqdn, avk, srv, ek, ss, eseed, svk),
    In( <'rsp', 'join', srv, ct_e> ),
    JoinRsp(fqdn, ek_e, sse, ss, keys, anchors) ]
  --[ ClientDone(host, fqdn, srv, avk, keys) ]->
  [ C2(host, fqdn, avk, keys) ]
  // binding: body = senc(<keys, anchors>, h(<ss, sse, 'rsp', 'join'>)).
  //   The linear JoinRsp fact is the server's own record of what it
  //   sealed to this exchange's ephemeral key and under which shared
  //   secrets -- the decryption-by-fact idiom: only the server that
  //   answered (deriving ss by decapsulating the request's kem_ct and
  //   sse by encapsulating to ek_e = kem_pk(eseed)) can produce it, so
  //   matching kem_ct(kem_pk(eseed), sse) against the network and the
  //   fact against the server's record is exactly opening the sealed
  //   body under k' = h(ss || sse, 'rsp').

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Rekey (S6): the join exchange with a Kerberos authenticator made
// from the host's own keytab.  The response is wrapped under
// ss || ss_e, so a recording plus the old host key is not enough: the
// new key needs the client's ephemeral dk_e.  The ticket and channel
// binding are abstracted to the old keytab term carried in the sealed
// body; the KDC's view of the host's current keys is !ValidHostKey.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule host_rekey:
  [ C2(host, fqdn, avk, keys),
    !Adv(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek),
    Fr(~ss), Fr(~eseed) ]
  --[ RekeyReq(host, fqdn, ~ss) ]->
  [ R1(host, fqdn, avk, srv, ek, ~ss, ~eseed, svk),
    Encap(fqdn, kem_ct(ek, ~ss), ~ss),
    SealedReq(fqdn, kem_ct(ek, ~ss), <keys, h(<~ss, 'cb'>)>),
    Out( <'rekey', ek, srv, fqdn, kem_ct(ek, ~ss), kem_pk(~eseed)> ) ]

rule server_rekey:
  [ !Adv(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek),
    In( <'rekey', ek, srv, fqdn, ct, ek_e> ),
    Encap(fqdn, ct, ss),
    SealedReq(fqdn, ct, <oldkeys, cb>),
    !ValidHostKey(fqdn, oldkeys),
    Fr(~sse), Fr(~newkeys) ]
  --[ ServerRekeyAccept(srv, fqdn, ~newkeys, jseed) ]->
  [ !ValidHostKey(fqdn, ~newkeys),
    RekeyRsp(fqdn, ek_e, ~sse, ss, ~newkeys, avk),
    Out( <'rsp', 'rekey', srv, kem_ct(ek_e, ~sse)> ) ]
  // binding: req = <'rekey', ek, srv, fqdn, kem_ct(ek, ss), ek_e,
  //   senc(<oldkeys, cb>, h(<ss, 'req'>))> as in server_join; oldkeys
  //   is checked against the KDC's !ValidHostKey.

rule rekey_finish:
  [ R1(host, fqdn, avk, srv, ek, ss, eseed, svk),
    In( <'rsp', 'rekey', srv, ct_e> ),
    RekeyRsp(fqdn, ek_e, sse, ss, newkeys, anchors) ]
  --[ RekeyDone(host, fqdn, srv, avk, newkeys) ]->
  [ C2(host, fqdn, avk, newkeys) ]
  // binding: body = senc(<newkeys, anchors>, h(<ss, sse, 'rsp',
  //   'rekey'>)); the RekeyRsp fact as in client_finish.

// The KDC maps a host entry to its current keys: the keytab the join
// installed, so a rekey is possible from enrollment on.
rule kdc_host_keys:
  [ C2(host, fqdn, avk, keys) ]
  -->
  [ !ValidHostKey(fqdn, keys) ]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Sealed keytab / escrow (S7.1-S7.2), core shape.
//
// The client encapsulates a fresh share ss_r to the server's escrow
// key, registers H(ct_r); the server releases ss_r only for a
// registered binding of an enabled host.  The DEK needs both shares
// A (server) and B (device), so a stolen disk alone (no server
// access) opens nothing.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule escrow_setup:
  [ Fr(~eseed) ]
  -->
  [ !EscrowKey($srv, ~eseed, kem_pk(~eseed)) ]

rule seal_keytab:
  [ C2(host, fqdn, avk, keys), !EscrowKey(srv, eseed, eek),
    Fr(~a), Fr(~b), Fr(~rseed) ]
  --[ Sealed(host, fqdn, ~a, ~b, srv) ]->
  [ SealedFile(host, fqdn, ~a, ~b, srv, ~rseed),
    Registration(fqdn, kem_ct(eek, ~rseed), ~rseed),
    Out( <'sealed', kem_ct(eek, ~rseed), senc(~a, ~rseed)> ) ]

rule recover:
  [ !EscrowKey(srv, eseed, eek),
    In( <'sealed', ct, wr> ),
    Registration(fqdn, ct, rseed),
    !ValidHostKey(fqdn, keys),
    Fr(~tseed) ]
  --[ Release(srv, fqdn, rseed) ]->
  [ !Released(fqdn, rseed, ~tseed),
    Out( senc(rseed, ~tseed) ) ]
  // the server opens the binding ciphertext ct to the share rseed
  // with its escrow key (decapsulation by fact through Registration);
  // the reply is sealed under the fresh transport key ~tseed

// The unsealing host combines the two shares with the released
// binding secret; the transport key h(tseed) is the server's fresh
// response encryption, learned from the reply itself.
rule unseal:
  [ SealedFile(host, fqdn, a, b, srv, rseed),
    !Released(fqdn, rseed, tseed),
    In( senc(rseed, tseed) ) ]
  --[ Unsealed(host, fqdn, h(<a, b, rseed>)) ]->
  [ ]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Vouchers (S9): a blind-issued ML-DSA signature on the join message
// m replaces the OTP (auth_type 3).  Three roles:
//
//   issuer        holds the ML-DSA voucher key vsk; the realm's
//                 servers accept it through !VKey, created only next
//                 to an honest server's anchor -- the advertisement's
//                 signed kuipa_vouchers member.
//   provisioner   decides the host, group and lease, and either binds
//                 the voucher to the host's ephemeral key or leaves it
//                 a bearer credential.
//   host          joins with the signature.
//
// Blindness is structural and needs no rule: ml-dsa-blind signs the
// 64-byte mu in external-mu mode, so the issuer's only input is a
// hash and never sees realm, host or group.  The provisioner keeps
// (salt, sigma), which any FIPS 204 verifier checks as an ordinary
// signature on salt||m -- so the model issues sgn(<salt, m>, vsk)
// directly and the mu round trip is invisible on the wire.
//
// The linear fact Voucher(sig, salt, m, vsk) is both the signature's
// validity (only issuance under a realm-accepted key creates it) and
// the directory's spent-salt record (ipaKUVoucherSpent): consumed at
// the join, a voucher is worth one host.  Quotas and one-more
// unforgeability are issuer policy, out of scope.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule voucher_key_add:
  [ !Server(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek), Fr(~vsk) ]
  -->
  [ !VKey(~vsk, avk) ]
  // add-voucher-key (S11.2): the issuer's key, listed in the
  // advertisement's kuipa_vouchers member, which the anchor signs --
  // so a voucher key exists only for a realm an honest server's
  // anchor vouches for, and a rogue server's advertisement never
  // verifies one

// The enrollment string for a voucher join carries the pin only (no
// OTP is spent), and the host has already run kuipa voucher-key: the
// fresh ~eseed is the host's ephemeral private key, published as the
// ek_e the provisioner binds to.  !VHost ties the host to its FQDN
// for the issuance rules.
rule enroll_string_voucher:
  [ !Server(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek), Fr(~eseed) ]
  -->
  [ C0($host, $fqdn, 'none', avk),
    !VKeyHost($host, ~eseed, kem_pk(~eseed)),
    !VHost($host, $fqdn),
    Out( kem_pk(~eseed) ) ]

rule issue_voucher_bound:
  [ !VKeyHost(host, eseed, ek_e), !VHost(host, fqdn),
    !VKey(vsk, avk), Fr(~salt) ]
  --[ VoucherIssued(~salt, 'bound', fqdn, ek_e) ]->
  [ Voucher( sgn(<~salt, fqdn, $hg, ek_e>, vsk), ~salt,
             <fqdn, $hg, ek_e>, vsk ),
    Out( <fqdn, $hg, sgn(<~salt, fqdn, $hg, ek_e>, vsk)> ) ]
  // the provisioner binds the voucher to the host's ek_e: m =
  // <fqdn, hostgroup, binding>.  The signature is public from here
  // on (the provisioner hands it to the host over any channel), which
  // is why BoundVoucherKeytabSecret below holds even with the
  // voucher in the attacker's hands -- useless without the host's
  // ephemeral private key, exactly as S9 says.

rule issue_voucher_bearer:
  [ !VHost(host, fqdn), !VKey(vsk, avk), Fr(~salt) ]
  --[ VoucherIssued(~salt, 'bearer', fqdn, 'none') ]->
  [ Voucher( sgn(<~salt, fqdn, $hg, 'bearer'>, vsk), ~salt,
             <fqdn, $hg, 'bearer'>, vsk ),
    Out( <fqdn, $hg, sgn(<~salt, fqdn, $hg, 'bearer'>, vsk)> ) ]
  // no binding: an unbound voucher is a bearer credential until used

rule steal_voucher_key:
  [ !VKey(vsk, avk) ]
  -->
  [ Out(vsk) ]
  // a compromised issuer key: whoever holds vsk signs vouchers of
  // its own, exactly as a compromised signing key impersonates a
  // server (S13) -- the signature is the credential

// With a stolen issuer key the attacker mints vouchers itself:
// In(vsk) succeeds only after steal_voucher_key published the key, so
// this rule is the symbolic form of "signs with a key the realm
// accepts".
rule mint_voucher_stolen_key:
  [ !VKey(vsk, avk), !VHost(host, fqdn), In(vsk), Fr(~salt) ]
  --[ VoucherIssued(~salt, 'bearer', fqdn, 'none'),
      VoucherForged(~salt) ]->
  [ Voucher( sgn(<~salt, fqdn, $hg, 'bearer'>, vsk), ~salt,
             <fqdn, $hg, 'bearer'>, vsk ),
    Out( <fqdn, $hg, sgn(<~salt, fqdn, $hg, 'bearer'>, vsk)> ) ]

rule client_join_voucher:
  [ C0(host, fqdn, otp, avk),
    !Adv(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek),
    !VKeyHost(host, eseed, ek_e),
    In( sig ),
    Fr(~ss) ]
  -->
  [ VC1(host, fqdn, avk, srv, ek, ~ss, eseed, svk),
    Encap(fqdn, kem_ct(ek, ~ss), ~ss),
    Out( <'vjoin', ek, srv, fqdn, kem_ct(ek, ~ss), ek_e, sig> ) ]
  // the voucher join is the join exchange with the signature in place
  // of the sealed OTP: same pin and advertisement check (C0 and !Adv
  // unified against the pinned anchor), same KEM request.  The client
  // forwards the voucher it was given; the server-side rules decide
  // whether it is worth anything.  The OTP field of C0 is unused --
  // the enrollment string here only carries the pin.

rule server_voucher_join:
  [ !Adv(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek),
    !VKey(vsk, avk),
    In( <'vjoin', ek, srv, fqdn, ct, ek_e, sig> ),
    Voucher( sig, salt, <fqdn, hg, ek_e>, vsk ),
    Encap(fqdn, ct, ss),
    Fr(~sse), Fr(~keys) ]
  --[ ServerAccept(srv, fqdn, ~keys),
      VoucherJoin(srv, fqdn, salt, ek_e, ~keys) ]->
  [ JoinRsp(fqdn, ek_e, ~sse, ss, ~keys, avk),
    Out( <'rsp', 'join', srv, kem_ct(ek_e, ~sse)> ) ]
  // the bound case: the voucher's m names this request's ek_e (the
  // shared variable ek_e in the In pattern and the Voucher fact is
  // the binding check -- a signature bound to another key does not
  // unify), the signature verifies under the issuer key the realm
  // accepts (vsk of !VKey, tied to this advertisement's anchor), and
  // m names this request's fqdn (shared variable fqdn).  Consuming
  // the linear Voucher fact is the ipaKUVoucherSpent write plus the
  // host add: the salt is spent and the FQDN exists, so the same
  // voucher cannot enroll twice.

rule server_voucher_join_bearer:
  [ !Adv(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek),
    !VKey(vsk, avk),
    In( <'vjoin', ek, srv, fqdn, ct, ek_e, sig> ),
    Voucher( sig, salt, <fqdn, hg, 'bearer'>, vsk ),
    Encap(fqdn, ct, ss),
    Fr(~sse), Fr(~keys) ]
  --[ ServerAccept(srv, fqdn, ~keys),
      VoucherJoin(srv, fqdn, salt, 'bearer', ~keys) ]->
  [ JoinRsp(fqdn, ek_e, ~sse, ss, ~keys, avk),
    Out( <'rsp', 'join', srv, kem_ct(ek_e, ~sse)> ) ]
  // the bearer case: nothing ties the request's ek_e to the voucher,
  // so whoever holds the signature may enroll the host with a key of
  // their own -- Attack_UnboundVoucherIsBearer below shows it

rule voucher_client_finish:
  [ VC1(host, fqdn, avk, srv, ek, ss, eseed, svk),
    In( <'rsp', 'join', srv, ct_e> ),
    JoinRsp(fqdn, kem_pk(eseed), sse, ss, keys, avk) ]
  --[ VoucherClientDone(host, fqdn, srv, avk, keys) ]->
  [ C2(host, fqdn, avk, keys) ]
  // the response opens under ss || sse with sse decapsulated by the
  // host's ephemeral key (the JoinRsp fact's kem_pk(eseed) is the
  // decryption-by-fact record, as in client_finish);  the join
  // installs like an online one

// The attacker as a voucher client with a bearer voucher: it holds
// the signature (Out'd at issuance), makes its own join request and
// its own ephemeral key, and the server -- unable to tell who
// encapsulated ct -- answers as for any other join (its request
// matches server_voucher_join_bearer's In pattern, and the JoinRsp
// that rule emits targets kem_pk(~eseed)).  Since both shared
// secrets and the decapsulation key are the attacker's own, the
// response payload -- the host's keytab -- lands in its knowledge.
rule attacker_bearer_join:
  [ !Adv(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek),
    !VKey(vsk, avk),
    In( <fqdn, sig> ),
    Fr(~ss), Fr(~eseed), Fr(~keys) ]
  --[ AttackerVoucherJoinDone(fqdn, ~keys, srv) ]->
  [ Out( <'vjoin', ek, srv, fqdn, kem_ct(ek, ~ss), kem_pk(~eseed), sig> ),
    Out(~keys) ]
  // the attacker's request on the wire: the server-side bearer rule
  // will still consume the Voucher fact and emit a JoinRsp to
  // kem_pk(~eseed); the attacker holds ~ss, ~eseed and the response
  // it decapsulates itself, so the keytab lands in its knowledge.
  // The premise In(<fqdn, sig>) is the voucher it learned from the
  // Out'd issuance; a bound voucher would name an honest host's ek_e
  // and never unify with this request's kem_pk(~eseed) at the
  // server, so only bearer vouchers reach the compromise.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// User seals (S7.6), the two-share file format of S7.6.4:
//
//   A, B: server and device shares;  DEK = h(<a, b>)
//   server slot:  (B_i, ss_i) = tang-pqc provision to eek;  the
//     binding secret r stands for ss_i and the provision's random,
//     so W = senc(A, r) and the commitment t = h(r, 'commit')
//   device slot:  (c, s) = ML-KEM.Encaps(dpk);  U = senc(B, s)
//
// One server slot and one device slot abstract IPUF's lists.  The
// registered device list is the trusted fact !Device;  the seal
// descriptor signature (S7.6.3) is a client-cache property outside
// the symbolic model, as are the revocation windows (S13 "Clock").
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule register_device:
  [ Fr(~dseed) ]
  -->
  [ !Device($user, ~dseed, kem_pk(~dseed)) ]

rule steal_device_key:
  [ !Device(user, dseed, dpk) ]
  --[ StolenDeviceKey(dpk) ]->
  [ Out(dseed) ]
  // a compromised device decapsulation key: the thief reads the
  // device slot and any release answer sent to that device

rule steal_escrow_key:
  [ !EscrowKey(srv, eseed, eek) ]
  --[ StolenEscrowKey(eek) ]->
  [ Out(eseed) ]
  // a compromised escrow key (an IPA administrator): the thief
  // decapsulates the file's server slot

// The escrow-key holder's decapsulation, as a rule (the equation
// chain kem_dec(kem_sk(eseed), kem_ct(eek, r)) then sdec(senc(a, r))
// = r then a): with eseed the thief opens every file's server slot
// sealed to this key, exactly the documented capability of the realm
// (S7.6.10's escrow row) -- the device share is what it lacks.
rule escrow_key_thief:
  [ !EscrowKey(srv, eseed, eek),
    !SealedUserFile(user, a, b, r, ct_d, eek), In(eseed) ]
  --[ EscrowCompromised(user, srv, a) ]->
  [ Out(a) ]

// The device-key holder's decapsulation, as a rule: with dseed the
// thief opens the file's device slot (b) and, given a release answer
// for a binding of this seal, decapsulates the answer's fresh
// encapsulation to the same device and recovers r -- with the server
// slot on the file (senc(a, r) on the wire) that yields a, and the
// DEK with it.  This is S7.6.10's "stolen device plus observed
// unlock" row, the bound on SealNeedsDeviceKey.
rule device_key_thief:
  [ !Device(user, dseed, dpk),
    !SealedUserFile(user, a, b, r, ct_d, eek),
    !ReleasedUser(user, r, ansseed, dpk), In(dseed) ]
  --[ DeviceCompromised(user, h(<a, b>)) ]->
  [ Out(h(<a, b>)) ]

rule user_seal:
  [ !Device(user, dseed, dpk), !EscrowKey(srv, eseed, eek),
    Fr(~a), Fr(~b), Fr(~r), Fr(~s) ]
  -->
  [ !SealedUserFile(user, ~a, ~b, ~r, kem_ct(dpk, ~s), eek),
    !SealBinding(user, ~r),
    Out( <'IPUF', h(~r, 'commit'), kem_ct(eek, ~r),
          senc(~a, ~r), kem_ct(dpk, ~s), senc(~b, ~s)> ) ]
  // the file on the wire: the commitment t, the server slot's
  // ciphertext and W, the device slot's ciphertext and U.  Neither
  // share is readable without the matching key: r needs the escrow
  // key (or a server release), s needs the device key.

// The owner asks for a release: the tang-pqc RecoveryRequest carries
// the binding whose commitment the file shows, and a factor the
// server checks (ticket, passkey or session -- abstracted here as
// the owner's own action through its device;  the device key is the
// load-bearing part of the policy, S7.6.5).
rule user_request:
  [ !Device(user, dseed, dpk),
    !SealedUserFile(user, a, b, r, ct_d, eek) ]
  -->
  [ Request(user, r) ]

// The unified recovery request, server side: decapsulation by fact
// (!SealBinding), the commitment check (r unified against the file),
// then the answer -- r under a fresh ML-KEM encapsulation to the
// named device's dpk, so only that device can read it.
rule user_unlock:
  [ !EscrowKey(srv, eseed, eek), !Device(user, dseed, dpk),
    !SealedUserFile(user, a, b, r, ct_d, eek), !SealBinding(user, r),
    Request(user, r),
    Fr(~ansseed) ]
  --[ SealRelease(user, srv, r) ]->
  [ !ReleasedUser(user, r, ~ansseed, dpk),
    Out( <'ans', kem_ct(dpk, ~ansseed), senc(r, ~ansseed)> ) ]
  // the answer's key is HKDF(ansseed, request hash) -- modeled as
  // ansseed itself;  repeated unlocks (S7.6.7) are repeated requests,
  // each with a fresh encapsulation

rule user_unseal:
  [ !SealedUserFile(user, a, b, r, ct_d, eek), !Device(user, dseed, dpk),
    !ReleasedUser(user, r, ansseed, dpk),
    In( senc(r, ansseed) ) ]
  --[ UserUnsealed(user, h(<a, b>)) ]->
  [ ]
  // the device decapsulates the answer with dseed (the answer's key
  // is derived from the encapsulation to its dpk, so matching the
  // ReleasedUser record against the wire ciphertext is exactly
  // opening it), opens its own slot for B (the fact carries it: only
  // the device's key holder acts here), and derives DEK = h(a, b)


// Lemmas
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

// Source lemma: every encapsulation secret ss the attacker learns was
// encapsulated under a join key whose seed the attacker holds (a rogue
// server); honest join-key secrets stay secret.
lemma SsSecretSource:
  "All srv ask avk ssk svk jseed ek #i.
    (!Server(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek) @i
     & not(srv = 'rogue'))
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(jseed) @j)"

lemma AttackerSsOnlyFromRogueSeeds:
  "All fqdn ct ss avk srv ask ssk svk jseed ek #k #l.
    (Encap(fqdn, ct, ss) @k
     & (!Server(srv, ask, avk, ssk, svk, jseed, ek) @l)
     & not(srv = 'rogue'))
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(ss) @j)"

// The protocol runs: a host completes an OTP join through an honest
// server, then rekeys, then seals and recovers its keytab.
lemma Exec: exists-trace
  "Ex host fqdn srv avk keys #i. ClientDone(host, fqdn, srv, avk, keys) @i"

// S13: the OTP travels only under the join-key shared secret; with an
// honest, unstolen pin the attacker never learns it.
lemma OtpSecret:
  "All host fqdn otp avk #i #k.
    Enroll(host, fqdn, otp, avk) @i & HonestAnchor(avk) @k
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(otp) @j)"

// S13: the keytab is delivered under ss || ss_e; a network attacker
// does not obtain the host's long-term keys.
lemma KeytabSecret:
  "All host fqdn srv avk keys #i #k.
    ClientDone(host, fqdn, srv, avk, keys) @i & HonestAnchor(avk) @k
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(keys) @j)"

// S5.1: ss authenticates the server implicitly.  Whenever a host with
// an honest pin completes a join, an honest server holding the
// advertised join key accepted the request and issued exactly that
// keytab.
lemma JoinServerAuth:
  "All host fqdn srv avk keys #i #k.
    ClientDone(host, fqdn, srv, avk, keys) @i & HonestAnchor(avk) @k
    ==> Ex srv2 #j. ServerAccept(srv2, fqdn, keys) @j"

// S6: forward secrecy of rekey -- the new keytab stays secret against
// the network attacker (a recording plus the old host key is not
// enough: the new key needs the client's ephemeral dk_e).
lemma RekeyForwardSecrecy:
  "All srv fqdn newkeys jseed #i.
    ServerRekeyAccept(srv, fqdn, newkeys, jseed) @i
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(newkeys) @j)"

// S7: escrow release only for registered, enabled hosts -- a stolen
// disk image alone (no server access) does not open a sealed keytab.
lemma SealedNoOffline:
  "All host fqdn a b srv #i.
    Sealed(host, fqdn, a, b, srv) @i
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(h(<a, b>)) @j)"

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Falsifiable sanity checks (expected: falsified, i.e. attacks found).
// They show the verified lemmas above are conditioned on the honest
// pin and are not vacuous: the same protocol *does* fail when the pin
// is wrong or the anchor is stolen, exactly as DESIGN S13 rows say.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

// A mis-delivered enrollment string (the attacker's anchor) lets the
// attacker's rogue server complete the join and learn the OTP.
lemma Attack_RoguePinLearnsOtp:
  "All host fqdn otp avk #i.
    Enroll(host, fqdn, otp, avk) @i
    & not(Ex #j. HonestAnchor(avk) @j)
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(otp) @j)"

// A stolen anchor (steal_anchor fired for the pinned key) exposes the
// OTP of an anchor-only (--ldap-otp) join: the thief impersonates the
// server.
lemma Attack_StolenAnchorLearnsOtp:
  "All host fqdn otp avk #i.
    Enroll(host, fqdn, otp, avk) @i
    & (Ex #j. StolenAnchor(avk) @j)
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(otp) @j)"

// With the escrow key compromised (attacker holds eseed), a stolen
// disk image opens: the escrow seal alone does not protect against the
// realm itself, which is why the two-share DEK exists (S7.6).
lemma Attack_EscrowKeyOpensDisk:
  "All host fqdn a b srv #i.
    Sealed(host, fqdn, a, b, srv) @i
    & (Ex srv2 eseed eek #j. !EscrowKey(srv2, eseed, eek) @j & K(eseed) @j)
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(h(<a, b>)) @j)"

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Voucher lemmas (S9)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

// The voucher flow runs end to end: a bound voucher enrolls its host
// and installs the keytab (the response opens under the host's own
// ephemeral key).
lemma VoucherExec: exists-trace
  "Ex host fqdn srv avk keys #i.
     VoucherClientDone(host, fqdn, srv, avk, keys) @i"

// A voucher is worth one host: two accepted voucher joins never
// spent the same salt under the same issuer key -- the directory's
// ipaKUVoucherSpent record and the host add refuse the second.
// Unforgeability is the linear fact: only issuance under a
// realm-accepted key creates a Voucher.
lemma VoucherSingleUse:
  "All srv1 fqdn1 salt1 b1 keys1 srv2 fqdn2 salt2 b2 keys2 #i #j.
    VoucherJoin(srv1, fqdn1, salt1, b1, keys1) @i
    & VoucherJoin(srv2, fqdn2, salt2, b2, keys2) @j
    & not(#i = #j)
    ==> not(salt1 = salt2)"

// Whenever a voucher join is accepted, the issuer key of this realm
// signed a voucher naming exactly this join's fqdn and (if bound)
// the request's ek_e: the host group and lease come from the issuer
// and the provisioner, and a bound voucher was issued for this very
// host key.
lemma VoucherIssuerAuth:
  "All srv fqdn salt b keys #i.
    VoucherJoin(srv, fqdn, salt, b, keys) @i
    ==> Ex salt2 kind fqdn2 ek_e #j.
      (VoucherIssued(salt2, kind, fqdn2, ek_e) @j & fqdn2 = fqdn)"
  // b (the voucher's binding, ek_e or 'bearer') is implied by the
  // issued kind:  kind = 'bound' means ek_e, 'bearer' means none.
  // Guarded: fqdn is bound by the VoucherJoin premise.

// A bound voucher in the attacker's hands (a copied signature, S9's
// "useless to anyone without the host's ephemeral private key") does
// not give it the host's keytab.
lemma BoundVoucherKeytabSecret:
  "All host fqdn srv avk keys #i #k.
    VoucherClientDone(host, fqdn, srv, avk, keys) @i
    & HonestAnchor(avk) @k
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(keys) @j)"

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// User seal lemmas (S7.6)
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

// The seal flow runs end to end: a file sealed with both factors
// opens through a server release to the registered device.
lemma SealExec: exists-trace
  "Ex user a b #i. UserUnsealed(user, h(<a, b>)) @i"

// A release happens only for a registered binding of that user: the
// server decapsulated a tang-pqc request whose binding the sealing
// recorded (the commitment check of S7.6.5 ties the request to this
// file), and the answer went only to the device key.  Without a
// server-side release there is no way to the server share -- the
// offline-resistance row of S7.6.10.
lemma SealReleaseRegistered:
  "All user r ansseed dpk #i.
    !ReleasedUser(user, r, ansseed, dpk) @i
    ==> Ex a b ct_d srv eek #j.
      ( !SealedUserFile(user, a, b, r, ct_d, eek) @j
        & SealRelease(user, srv, r) @j )"

// The two-share property (S7.6.4, S7.6.10): while the device key is
// uncompromised, the DEK stays closed -- against the network attacker
// holding a copy of the file, against the realm's escrow key (the IPA
// administrator row: the server share alone yields nothing), and
// against observed release answers (the answer's key is under a fresh
// encapsulation to the device).  Every path to the DEK needs b, and b
// is under the device slot alone.
lemma SealNeedsDeviceKey:
  "All user a b r ct_d eek dseed dpk #i #k.
    ( !SealedUserFile(user, a, b, r, ct_d, eek) @i
      & !Device(user, dseed, dpk) @k
      & not(Ex #j. K(dseed) @j) )
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(h(<a, b>)) @j)"

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Falsifiable sanity checks, seal and voucher rows (expected:
// falsified).  They show the seal lemmas are conditioned on the
// device factor and both shares, and that the model reproduces the
// S9/S7.6.10 attacks the design predicts.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

// A bearer voucher enrolls whoever holds it: the attacker's own
// request (its own ek_e and ss) is accepted, and it reads the
// keytab.  This is S9's "an unbound voucher is a bearer credential
// until used".
lemma Attack_UnboundVoucherIsBearer:
  "All salt fqdn2 #i.
    VoucherIssued(salt, 'bearer', fqdn2, 'none') @i
    ==> not(Ex fqdn keys srv #j. AttackerVoucherJoinDone(fqdn, keys, srv) @j)"

// The realm's escrow key yields the server share: an IPA
// administrator (eseed compromised) decapsulates the file's server
// slot (r from kem_ct(eek, r), then A from senc(A, r)) and reads A.
// This bounds SealNeedsDeviceKey from below: the administrator gets
// exactly one share, and the DEK needs both (S7.6.10's escrow row).
lemma Attack_EscrowKeyYieldsServerShare:
  "All user srv a #i.
    EscrowCompromised(user, srv, a) @i
    ==> not(Ex #k. K(a) @k)"

// A stolen device key plus the observed release answer opens the
// file: the thief decapsulates the answer with the stolen device key
// (r is under ansseed, encrypted to dpk) and combines the shares.
// This bounds SealClosedWithoutUnlock: it holds only while the
// device key is uncompromised.
lemma Attack_StolenDevicePlusReleaseOpensSeal:
  "All user dek #i.
    DeviceCompromised(user, dek) @i
    ==> not(Ex #l. K(dek) @l)"


end
